  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.30.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.30.0 ships a fifth optional MCP server, jira, reaching an organisation issue tracker through the remote server Atlassian itself hosts. It needed no new mechanism: the remote form already renders exactly what a working hand-written entry for that server contains, and no credential travels in the descriptor. Three facts this distribution has to carry rather than discover. First, the runtime does not authenticate a remote server on its own: it marks the server as needing auth and raises a toast, and a person has to run opencode mcp auth jira once or no tool answers. The doctor command cannot see this either, because a remote server is reported as remote and never started. Second, nothing is withheld. What that server exposes is Atlassian to decide, and an agent granted it can create, transition and edit, not only read. The tool list is not enumerable without credentials, and a name guessed wrong in a deny list withholds nothing while looking like it does, so the whole of the judgment lives in the shipped convention instead, which carries an explicit default for the ambiguous case: when in doubt do not write, say what you would have written. Six of the eight agents that receive it are sub-agents with no way to ask, so for them that resolves in one direction only. Third, anyone already administering their own jira key has a collision: a self-granted key reaches every agent, the shipped server reaches eight, and the install that selects it drops the carried-forward grant silently. The same release repairs two statements that had gone false: the manual taught granting by hand the very key the product now ships, and a whole test module used jira as its example of a server the engine never heard of, passing while documenting the opposite of the truth. This distribution changed nothing of its own, as in 1.6.0 and 1.7.0: the work happened upstream and only the pin moved."
  }
}
